A||||22||camon||rer end rattle!||||5||
Z||000000||camon||11-23-2001||08:08 PM||||For the past few weeks I have noticed a rattle (when the windows are down and I hit bumps). This rattle I first thought was coming from the front driver side....so I removed the wheels (good thing I did...the front shocks were shot, so I replaced them) I checked everything up front to see if anything was loose....well there wasn't anything loose. After I had the shocks on the front I took it for a spin and it still rattled. Well today I was replacing speakers and while standing at the rear driver side wheel I accidently kicked the rear wheel and I heard the rattle (so I have found where it is coming from). Well I ran out of day light after getting the speakers in so I couldn't pull the driver side rear wheel (I'll do it Saturday).<P>Well my question is does anyone know what is making the noise???<BR>Like I said earlier it is making a rattling noise when I hit bumps (almost like something is loose). the noise can be heard if the driver side tire is kicked as well. The passenger side makes no noise nor do any of the other wheels/tires.<P>So anyone know what it could be prior to me removing the wheel?<P><BR>Thanks! <P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||5
Z||000001||bocefas||11-23-2001||08:38 PM||||My bet would be the dust shield on the back of the rotor, perhaps it is loose.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dan010||11-24-2001||12:29 AM||||Sounds just like the same rattle/noise<BR>that I have, 86 2.8 gt.<BR>Have been working on other issues first, but this is coming up fast on the list of thing to look into.<BR> Please post what you find out! I will do<BR>the same should I get to it soon!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||camon||11-24-2001||01:10 AM||||Thanks for the shield info...i'll check into it tommorow when I go looking around the rear end!<P>Dan:<BR>I will be sure to post the problem (what and where it was) tommorow in this thread after I get it fixed. I really don't think it is anything serious (fingers crossed). It just sounds like something is loose on that wheel.<P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fierowrecker||11-24-2001||11:01 AM||||Sounds like loose brake pads to me...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dan010||11-24-2001||11:11 AM||||Went exploreing this morning, my noise is diffently from the front end drivers side,<BR>started by kicking the rear tire as you did.<BR> Heard noise, but from the front, jacked up <BR>front, grabbed tire and wiggled, hard to fine exactly where noise is comming from, not much play at all.<BR>Could I just need a good lub at the ball joints and tie rod ends ect.||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||11-24-2001||11:27 AM||||Be sure that both bolts that hold on the brake caliper are present...<P>When I bought my Fiero a few years back, it made a "clanging" noise when I hit bumps. I discovered one of the rear calipers was missing a mounting bolt and the caliper was hitting the hub every time I hit a bump.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eriamjh||11-24-2001||12:42 PM||||Check your exhaust for all the springs.||||reg||1
Z||000008||sqoach||11-24-2001||01:02 PM||||I had a rattle in the front driver's side and it turned out to be a loose inner brake pad. The front passenger side had a loose caliper bolt. Just check everything and make sure it's tight.<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigsqoach2.gif">              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">               <BR>^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 SC 2.5L 5spd</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mach10||11-24-2001||01:38 PM||||Rusted-off rattle clips?||||reg||5
Z||000010||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-24-2001||01:59 PM||||A dab of heat resistant sillicone does wonders to rattling brake pads if the rattle clips are broke or bent bad  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I kept both of our cars quiet since we live on a bumpy country road  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Dan010||11-24-2001||04:27 PM||||Well you guys were right again for mine, the retaining clip was bent so it was loose in the piston, when I took the caliper, the inner pad nearly fell out. Bent the taps a little, re-assembled, and not a quite as can be. But I think I do have some other front end issues to fix later, like ball joints and tie rod ends, fun fun.<P>I hope you fine yours to be as simple as mine turned out to be!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000012||camon||11-24-2001||07:07 PM||||Well i've pulled the rear driverside wheel and found my problem. However I don't know how to correct it (i'm sure someone on here will though).<P>Before pulling the wheel with car jacked up I checked for worn wheel bearings (good news that isn't it). I then countinued by taking off the rear tire/wheel and started to grab and pull anything I could get my hands on to locate what was loose. Well nothing seemed loose until I grabed the brake rotor. Well this is where the noise is coming from.<BR>If I grab the rotor and wiggle it there is a little play their.<P>What is causing this. I have read the hayes manual and found out that the only thing that holds the rotor on is the brake calipers and wheel/lug nuts. Well if this is the case then why is it rattling even when the wheel and lugs are on and tight? Could it be caused by worn out brake pads???? I have them (bought them when I replaced the shot front pads) but didn't replace the rears at that time because the rear pads still looked pretty descent.<P>So anyone know??????? <P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||1
Z||000013||Dan010||11-25-2001||08:50 AM||||Did you get your problem fixed?||||reg||1
Z||000014||camon||11-25-2001||01:33 PM||||No I haven't Dan!<P>As explained above I know it is the rotor causing the rattle (it seems loose). The hayes manual states that the wheel/lugs and the caliper is what hold the rotor on tight.  Well the caliper is tight (it don't move) and the lugs/wheel is tight and the rotor still moves.<P>Why is this rotor loose and what do I do to make the rotor tight?<P>Like I said the caliper is tight (no movement) but the rotor still has play in it.<BR>Anyone??????? worn Pads, shot caliper....Anyone???????<P>------------------<BR>Keith<p>[This message has been edited by camon (edited 11-25-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000015||sqoach||11-25-2001||02:31 PM||||camon, when you take the wheel off (rear), the rotor is going to be a little loose. It should be on tight when the wheel is on. I'd try to see if you can wiggle the rotor around when the wheel is on. Make sure your lugnuts are tight. If the wheel is on tight, the brake pads have nothing to do with the rotor movement. The wheel is what holds it on.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Dan010||11-25-2001||04:50 PM||||Could it be as with mine, that the pads are loose and if so, it will rattle a lot, and quite loud.||||reg||1
Z||000017||camon||11-25-2001||04:55 PM||||That is what I thought.......<BR>If the wheel is tight then the rotor has to be tight.<P>When it finally stops raining I will need to see if the rotor is moving while the wheel is on (now that I think about it...I doubt it would).<P>That would mean the only thing left would be the caliper mounting. But then it seemed tight yesterday when I tried to wiggle it (no movement what so ever).<P>Anything else that it could be? I know it is something with the driveside rear wheel assembly because it is the only wheel that makes the noise. I don't hear any grinding or anything just a metal rattling noise when I either hit bumps or kick that tire!<P>Thanks!!!<P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||1
Z||000018||camon||11-25-2001||05:00 PM||||Dan:<BR>Could be....I didn't check the pads, only the caliper mounting.<P>I will need to check it out if it stops raining tonight sometime or else I will need to put it off til next week end because I won't have the time during the week (ohh I hate working second shift)!<P>Again thanks to all for the input!<P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||12
Z||000019||sqoach||11-25-2001||08:54 PM||||Try using your brakes when you go over a bump. If the sound is gone, then, most likely your brake pads are loose (probably the inner one). If you still hear the sound, it could be that your caliper piston is not working, causing the pad to still rattle around. Do your brakes work good?||||reg||1
Z||000020||camon||11-26-2001||01:56 AM||||They seem to stop me as well as any other car I have had.<P>I will check the rattle noise tommorow when I go to work (go over a bump...hit the brakes and see if it rattles). <P>Thanks for the advice! <P><P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||12
Z||000021||stsmithgt||11-26-2001||10:33 AM||||I had a rattle like that and it turned out to be a loose spring on my exhaust.  One was tight and the other had no load at the same point on the pipe.  I guess the new system put in was a little off track.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000022||camon||11-26-2001||01:46 PM||||Man you guys know your stuff ...i'll tell you what. This has to be the best board on the net for info and trouble shooting problems on cars .....fiero's in general (what would we do without it)!!!!<P><BR>Well Sqoach and Dan......<BR>It is the brake pads making the noise (same as your problem Dan). I just got back from taking it for a quick spin. When I went over bumps I litely applied the brakes and there was no rattle heard, went over some bumps with no brakes applied and there was the rattle.....so it is the pads. <P>So this coming weekend I will rip into this rear brake job. Since I have new pads for the rear that I just haven't got around to installing yet...this will be a good time to do it as well. I have a hayes manual that will assist me in the job (any heads up calls on the job that hayes may have forgot to mention).<P>::Thanks Sqoach, Dan and everyone for the help with this brake problem and other things that I have had trouble with....I have learned more from this board then I could have ever hoped for::<P><BR>Thanks!!!!<P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||14
